---
description: Find, assess, and resolve worthwhile duplicate code
---

Treat `$ARGUMENTS` as the code target, not a Beads scope. Resolve it to the requested path(s); ask only if missing or ambiguous. Scan only that target, using the repository root only when the whole project is requested.

Run local jscpd with the managed config and compact reporter:

```sh
jscpd --config "$HOME/.config/opencode/jscpd.json" --reporters ai --no-tips --fail-on-empty -- "<resolved-target>"
```

Replace the placeholder with the resolved path; for multiple targets, pass each as a separate quoted argument. Keep the managed exclusions and local-only detection: do not substitute project configuration, use MCP or remote analysis services, or enable embeddings or downloads.

Read reported source fragments and nearby context to assess whether each finding is genuinely equivalent and worth simplifying. Report clone locations, actual scope and material limitations, including excluded code, unavailable cross-target matches, scan failures, empty analysis or truncated output. Do not treat incomplete results as a complete scan.

For each candidate, assign a 1–3 value/feasibility score: 3 means a clear, worthwhile change; 2 means a genuine unresolved trade-off that needs user or architectural judgment; 1 means leave it alone, and ignore it for execution. Explain the score briefly. Preserve intentional repetition and never turn superficial similarity into an abstraction.

If there are no 2 or 3 candidates, report that nothing worthwhile is approved and stop without asking for confirmation or editing. Otherwise, show a concise summary of all 3s. When any 2s exist, ask a multi-select question listing those genuine trade-offs and the relevant options, with a recommendation where useful. Ask the user to confirm proceeding with all 3s and only the 2s they select; do not execute before that confirmation. On confirmation, create a self-contained execution brief for the approved work: identify the concrete duplication and relevant files/code, state the intended outcome and constraints, and do not mention scores or depend on the assessment context. If many findings warrant it, group the work into manageable waves that account for overlap and dependencies; do not add waves for a small set. Implement only the approved work within the requested target, then summarize the changes and any remaining findings. Do not execute 1s or unselected 2s. If the user approves no candidates, stop without editing.
